DOCUMENT();

loadMacros("PGstandard.pl", "PGML.pl", "parserRadioButtons.pl");

TEXT(beginproblem());

$mc1 = RadioButtons(["Insert incorrect answer here", "Insert another incorrect answer here", "Insert yet another incorrect answer here", "Insert correct answer here"], "Insert correct answer here");

$mc2 = RadioButtons(["Insert incorrect answer here", "Line 3. Note that \(\arcsin(0) = 0\) which is not in \(U\).", "Insert another incorrect answer here", "Insert yet another incorrect answer here"], "Line 3. Note that \(\arcsin(0) = 0\) which is not in \(U\).");

BEGIN_PGML
Let [` F: \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R} `] be [` F(x) = \sin x `]. Fardin and Amy try to prove [`F`] is a local diffeomorphism at [`\pi`].

Fardin carelessly writes two short lines.

---

1. _Note [`\sin x`] is [`C^1`] and its inverse [`\arcsin x`] is [`C^1`]._
2. _Thus, [`F`] is a local diffeomorphism at [`\pi`]._

---

Explain why his proof is severely flawed.

[_]{$mc1}

Amy does better, but also makes a serious mistake in one line.

---

1. _Note [`F(x) = \sin x`] is [`C^1`]._
2. _Also, [`F(x) = \sin x`] bijectively maps [`U = (\frac{\pi}{2}, \frac{3\pi}{2})`] to [`V = (-1,1)`]._
3. _The inverse map is [`G(x) = \arcsin x`]._
4. _Note [`G`] is [`C^1`], since [`G'(x) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}`] is continuous for [`-1 < x < 1`]._
5. _Thus, [`F`] is a local diffeomorphism at [`\pi`]._

---

Identify the flawed line and briefly explain what is wrong.

[_]{$mc2}
END_PGML

$showHint = 1;
BEGIN_HINT
For Fardin, what are the domain and range of arcsin? Note that “inverses” of trigonometric functions are inverse functions of specific restrictions of the trigonometric functions.
END_HINT

$showPartialCorrectAnswers = 0;

ENDDOCUMENT();